C = [
("h1", "8. The Nations That Come"),
("p", "Claim: the open clauses of the oracles against the nations are not loose ends. They join the prophets’ other word about the nations, that in the last days they come to Zion to be taught, to worship and to seek the LORD, and that Israel stands among them with a name. The volume holds the open oracles as one picture: nations blessed through Israel’s restored line in the Kingdom, each still named as the nation it is, with Jerusalem at the centre. It does not read the Body of Christ or the church into any of these passages, and it does not read them as already done."),
("sub", "8.1 The mountain of the LORD’s house"),
("p", "Isaiah 2 is the prophets’ plainest picture of the nations coming. “And it shall come to pass in the last days, that the mountain of the LORD’s house shall be established in the top of the mountains, and shall be exalted above the hills; and all nations shall flow unto it” (Isaiah 2:2). The people’s speech is in the next verse: “Come ye, and let us go up to the mountain of the LORD, to the house of the God of Jacob; and he will teach us of his ways, and we will walk in his paths: for out of Zion shall go forth the law, and the word of the LORD from Jerusalem” (Isaiah 2:3). The nations travel to a place, to a house, and to a teaching that comes out of a city. Then the judging: “And he shall judge among the nations, and shall rebuke many people: and they shall beat their swords into plowshares, and their spears into pruninghooks: nation shall not lift up sword against nation, neither shall they learn war any more” (Isaiah 2:4)."),
("p", "The phrase “the last days” matters for the volume’s reading of the clauses in Jeremiah. The King James translators render one Hebrew phrase by “last days” in Isaiah 2:2 and by “latter days” in Jeremiah 48:47 and 49:39. The volume draws no date from the phrase and no more than a link. The captivities of Moab and Elam are brought again in the same stretch of time that Isaiah gives to the nations’ going up to Zion, and the clause for Moab sits beside a sentence about a throne in the tabernacle of David (Isaiah 16:5). The restoration of the nations and the establishment of Zion are told in one season."),
("sub", "8.2 Egypt, Assyria and Israel"),
("p", "The passage this volume has called its hardest test of the reading is Isaiah 19:23–25, read now in the company of Isaiah 2. “In that day shall there be a highway out of Egypt to Assyria, and the Assyrian shall come into Egypt, and the Egyptian into Assyria, and the Egyptians shall serve with the Assyrians. In that day shall Israel be the third with Egypt and with Assyria, even a blessing in the midst of the land: whom the LORD of hosts shall bless, saying, Blessed be Egypt my people, and Assyria the work of my hands, and Israel mine inheritance” (Isaiah 19:23–25). The three names are kept. Egypt is still Egypt and Assyria is still Assyria, and Israel is the third. The blessing is “in the midst of the land,” which is the land of Israel in the other passages of the volume’s texts. The nations are not made into Israel, and Israel is not made into the nations."),
("p", "The same note sounds in Zephaniah, who, in the last chapter of his book, has the LORD say, “For then will I turn to the people a pure language, that they may all call upon the name of the LORD, to serve him with one consent” (Zephaniah 3:9). The suppliants come from far: “From beyond the rivers of Ethiopia my suppliants, even the daughter of my dispersed, shall bring mine offering” (Zephaniah 3:10). Earlier in the same prophet, in the oracle against the Philistines and Moab and Ammon, the LORD says, “The LORD will be terrible unto them: for he will famish all the gods of the earth; and men shall worship him, every one from his place, even all the isles of the heathen” (Zephaniah 2:11). The oracle on the nations holds the two sides together: the gods are starved and the nations worship. Habakkuk closes his taunt on the same two sides, “For the earth shall be filled with the knowledge of the glory of the LORD, as the waters cover the sea” (Habakkuk 2:14), and, six verses on, “let all the earth keep silence before him” (Habakkuk 2:20)."),
("sub", "8.3 Jerusalem at the centre"),
("p", "Zechariah speaks of the nations’ coming in terms that centre on a city and a people. “Yea, many people and strong nations shall come to seek the LORD of hosts in Jerusalem, and to pray before the LORD” (Zechariah 8:22). And then: “In those days it shall come to pass, that ten men shall take hold out of all languages of the nations, even shall take hold of the skirt of him that is a Jew, saying, We will go with you: for we have heard that God is with you” (Zechariah 8:23). Ten men from every language take hold of one Jew. The picture holds the people, the places, and the reason the nations come: they have heard that God is with them. The nations ask to go with Israel."),
("p", "Zechariah 14 gives the same coming in terms of those who survive. “And it shall come to pass, that every one that is left of all the nations which came against Jerusalem shall even go up from year to year to worship the King, the LORD of hosts, and to keep the feast of tabernacles” (Zechariah 14:16). The word “left” says that these are the survivors of nations that had come against the city. Then the penalty for not coming: “even upon them shall be no rain” (Zechariah 14:17), and Egypt is named: “And if the family of Egypt go not up, and come not, that have no rain; there shall be the plague, wherewith the LORD will smite the heathen that come not up to keep the feast of tabernacles” (Zechariah 14:18). The chapter ends on the holiness of the city: “Yea, every pot in Jerusalem and in Judah shall be holiness unto the LORD of hosts” (Zechariah 14:21)."),
("p", "Two points follow. The first is that the nations are still nations in these texts, with families, and Egypt is named, so a nation’s blessing does not erase it. The second is that judgment and blessing live in the same chapter. Egypt that is “my people” in Isaiah 19:25 is a family under the plague in Zechariah 14:18 if it does not come up. The volume does not harmonize the two by timeline. It reads them as the same kingdom seen from two sides: a kingdom in which the nations are blessed and ruled. The blessing is real and the rule is real."),
("sub", "8.4 Israel’s name among them"),
("p", "The oracles that restore the nations also restore Israel, and the prophets keep the two in step. Ammon’s fall is a sentence “then shall Israel be heir unto them that were his heirs” (Jeremiah 49:2). Ezekiel says of the neighbours that despised Israel, “they shall dwell safely therein, and shall build houses, and plant vineyards” after the LORD has “executed judgments upon all those that despise them round about them” (Ezekiel 28:26). Zephaniah closes his book on the same pairing: “At that time will I bring you again, even in the time that I gather you: for I will make you a name and a praise among all people of the earth, when I turn back your captivity before your eyes, saith the LORD” (Zephaniah 3:20). And Amos closes his on the tabernacle of David raised, so that the house of David “may possess the remnant of Edom, and of all the heathen, which are called by my name” (Amos 9:11, 12). The restoration of the nations is not a separate event from the restoration of Israel. In every one of these passages the nations are blessed through Israel’s restored line, and Israel is given a name among them."),
("p", "This is the point at which the volume refers to the promise to Abraham, “and in thee shall all families of the earth be blessed” (Genesis 12:3). The promise is read in Covenantal Volume 1, and the volume takes it up in one respect only: the open clauses of this volume are the kind of text in which Genesis 12:3 is spoken of in the prophets, nations blessed through the line the LORD has set in Zion. The volume does not read Genesis 12:3 or Galatians 3 further than that."),
("sub", "8.5 Where the Body is not read"),
("p", "The Body of Christ is not named in any passage of this chapter or of the oracles that precede it. The volume does not read the Body into them. The oracles are addressed to nations, their grounds are what nations did, and their open clauses are carried by Israel’s restored line in a kingdom with Jerusalem at its centre. The church is not the five cities of Egypt that speak the language of Canaan, it is not the mountain of the LORD’s house, and it is not the family of Egypt that goes up to Jerusalem or does not. This volume does not say that the Body of Christ has no blessing. It says that these passages are not the place the Body finds it. The Body is in no Israelite covenant, and the promises of the nations’ blessing are tied to Israel’s house and Zion."),
("ctbl", "The nations that come, passage by passage.", [
    ("Passage", "Who comes", "To what, and with whom"),
    ("Isaiah 2:2–4", "All nations, many people", "To the mountain of the LORD’s house; the law goes out of Zion"),
    ("Isaiah 19:23–25", "Egypt and Assyria", "A highway between them; Israel the third; three names kept"),
    ("Zephaniah 3:9, 10", "The people with a pure language; suppliants from beyond Ethiopia", "To call on the LORD’s name; an offering brought"),
    ("Zechariah 8:20–23", "Many people and strong nations; ten men of every language", "To seek the LORD in Jerusalem; they take hold of the skirt of a Jew"),
    ("Zechariah 14:16–21", "The survivors of the nations that came against Jerusalem; Egypt named", "To worship the King and keep the feast of tabernacles; no rain for those who do not"),
    ("Amos 9:11, 12", "The remnant of Edom and all the heathen called by the LORD’s name", "Possessed by the house of David raised up"),
  ], [0.22, 0.38, 0.4]),
("sub", "8.6 What the volume takes from the passages"),
("bul", [
    "Isaiah 2:2–4 puts the nations’ going up to Zion in “the last days,” the phrase that translates the same Hebrew as the “latter days” of Jeremiah 48:47 and 49:39; the volume draws a link and no date.",
    "Isaiah 19:23–25 keeps three names, Egypt, Assyria and Israel, and the blessing falls on each as itself.",
    "Zephaniah 3:9, 10 and Zechariah 8:20–23 and 14:16–21 describe nations coming to worship, with Jerusalem at the centre; Zechariah 14 names Egypt and its penalty.",
    "In every passage the restoration of the nations is joined to Israel’s, and Israel is given a name among them (Jeremiah 49:2; Ezekiel 28:26; Zephaniah 3:20; Amos 9:11, 12).",
    "The Body of Christ is not named in these passages and is not read into them; the open oracles are held open as nations blessed through Israel’s restored line in the Kingdom.",
  ]),
]
